.TH SDL_strtok_r 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_strtok_r \- This works exactly like strtok_r() but doesn\(aqt require access to a C runtime.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_stdinc.h>
.PP
.BI "char * SDL_strtok_r(char *str, const char *delim, char **saveptr);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Break a string up into a series of tokens\[char46]
.PP
To start tokenizing a new string,
.BR str
should be the non-NULL address of the string to start tokenizing\[char46] Future calls to get the next token from the same string should specify a NULL\[char46]
.PP
Note that this function will overwrite pieces of
.BR str
with null chars to split it into tokens\[char46] This function cannot be used with const/read-only strings!
.PP
.BR saveptr
just needs to point to a
.BR "char *"
that can be overwritten; SDL will use this to save tokenizing state between calls\[char46] It is initialized if
.BR str
is non-NULL, and used to resume tokenizing when
.BR str
is NULL\[char46]

.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I str
the string to tokenize, or NULL to continue tokenizing\[char46]
.TP
.I delim
the delimiter string that separates tokens\[char46]
.TP
.I saveptr
pointer to a char *, used for ongoing state\[char46]
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns A pointer to the next token, or NULL if no tokens remain\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
It is safe to call this function from any thread\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

